<p>Where bank branches can't reach remote areas, and where a debit card or a credit history is hard to come by, the South's answer lies not in the banking system but in mobile networks, QR codes, and increasingly open tools. It did not copy the North's path of "build branches first, then digitize"; instead it let financial and intelligent services reach far wider populations directly.</p>

<p>That same path underlies the AI road Africa is now walking.</p>

<h2>One. First, what "Global South" actually means</h2>

<p>"Global South" is not the area below the equator on a map. It is a political-economic concept: a group of countries with lower development levels, a shared colonial history, and a shared demand for change in the international order.</p>

<p>Its lineage can be traced to the left movements of the 1960s, and further back to French scholar Alfred Sauvy's "Third World" in 1952. After the Cold War, "Third World" faded for carrying a hierarchical tint, and "Global South" became the more neutral term.</p>

<p>Is China part of the Global South? In a white paper on global governance released in June 2026, China's official position identifies it as a member; yet China is also the world's second-largest economy, located in the Northern Hemisphere. Western academia is more divided, with some arguing "Global South" is a matter of identity rather than economic reality. This very dispute is a footnote to the concept: it is not a static map, but a group of people with "similar starting points and similar solutions," now helping define their own position.</p>

<h2>Two. Digital livelihoods: three legs of financial inclusion</h2>

<p>The South escapes financial exclusion not through bank branches but through three legs built on digital public infrastructure (DPI): payments, identity, and credit.</p>

<p><strong>Payments.</strong> India's UPI (Unified Payments Interface) is the flagship. According to the National Payments Corporation of India, UPI processed 23.66 billion transactions in July 2026 alone. For a vendor, a QR code stuck on the stall lets customers pay directly — no POS terminal, no change to make. Brazil's Pix is similar in scale, processing nearly 80 billion transactions in 2025, used by about 93% of adults. Africa's M-Pesa shows that a smartphone is not even necessary: a feature phone and a SIM card suffice to send money, pay bills, and save — with 40 million monthly active users in Kenya by March 2026.</p>

<p><strong>Identity.</strong> India's Aadhaar issues a unique identity number using fingerprints and iris scans; by 2026 it has issued roughly 1.4 billion numbers. Digitizing identity makes being "seen by the state" possible, and lets benefits, account opening, and subsidies reach individuals more precisely.</p>

<p><strong>Credit.</strong> Without collateral, use data instead. Payment and bill-payment records become the basis for credit assessment, giving small merchants and low-income workers — with no property and no traditional credit file — a first chance at formal credit.</p>

<p>The three legs share low barriers and broad reach. What do they change for ordinary people? An Indian tea-stall owner who once only took cash and couldn't borrow can now use UPI flow as a credit record and access small business loans; a Kenyan woman in a village who once faced long, costly remittance routes can now complete a transfer with one M-Pesa account.</p>

<p>One change is easy to overlook: people grow more capable while using these tools — learning bookkeeping, reading cash flow, understanding credit. Digital livelihoods offer not just "a pipeline for access" but "a field for practice."</p>

<p>But this is not a tech utopia. The digital divide persists: account ownership in sub-Saharan Africa is about 58%, below the global average of 79%; women's account ownership still trails men's; and when "credit" becomes a data string and "identity" becomes a fingerprint, those without phones, without skills, or unwilling to let face and iris into a database may be blocked by new thresholds. Convenience and risk have to be seen together.</p>

<h2>Three. Africa's AI path: go around the gap, not fill it first</h2>

<p>Africa's development bottleneck is often summed up as "inadequate infrastructure." But fifteen years of mobile payments have already demonstrated a different path: when bank branches couldn't reach people, Africa didn't spend decades building physical branches — it went straight to mobile. AI is offering a similar turning point.</p>

<p>The gaps are real. Africa's median age is under 20, yet one teacher facing 50 to 80 students is the norm; there are fewer than 0.3 doctors per thousand people, below the WHO's recommended standard; about 60% of the population works in smallholder farming but struggles to get accurate weather, soil, and market information.</p>

<p>AI's value lies in going around these gaps: personalized tutoring eases the scale bottleneck of basic education without building new schools or mass-hiring teachers; AI-assisted diagnosis and teleconsultation fill regions with "no doctors"; digital agriculture services reshape the decision chain of "what to plant, when to plant, where to sell."</p>

<p>Even more notable is a set of turning signals. According to Condia and TechCabal Insights, African startups raised $705 million in Q1 2026, up 26.5% year over year — against a backdrop of global VC contraction. GSMA projects Africa's digital economy will exceed $180 billion by 2030. Meanwhile Kenya, South Africa, and Nigeria have rolled out data localization rules, creating demand for locally built AI training datasets.</p>

<p>The way Chinese companies participate is also changing. Huawei builds 4G/5G networks across Africa and runs AI innovation labs in Egypt, South Africa, and Kenya for local adaptation; Transsion holds over 40% of Africa's smartphone market (IDC tracker), with its PalmPay pushing mobile payments further down; Alibaba Cloud runs nodes in South Africa and Nigeria and, through the eFounders Fellowship, helps African entrepreneurs build digital capability.</p>

<p>One key difference: AI infrastructure is not an extractive resource but a cumulative asset — the more data, the better the models, the richer the ecosystem. This differs fundamentally from traditional resource extraction; the working mode is "build together," not "teach them."</p>

<h2>Four. A clear-eyed view of a manuscript still being written</h2>

<p>Return to the 1.3 billion people without bank accounts. The Global North has mature banking systems and large models; the Global South is using "digital public infrastructure + mobile payments + affordable AI" to bring financial and intelligent services to wider populations. Neither path is superior — only the starting points differ.</p>

<p>What is worth watching in the South is not which narrative it flatters, but that it has begun answering "what is development, what is a good life" in its own way — measured by the most basic yardstick: whether ordinary people's lives actually improved.</p>

<p>The digital divide remains, risk remains, and the power to define is still under discussion. But the direction is clear: a rural woman opens her first account, a stall vendor is accepted into a formal payment network, a smallholder farmer asks an AI a question in their own language for the first time. These everyday, incremental, still-happening facts are the real clues to this path.</p>

